data.content:
Your code **does not use `Thread.Abort`**, `Thread.ResetAbort`, or catch `ThreadAbortException`. The APIs it does use (`Thread.Start`, `Thread.Join`, `Thread.Sleep`) work identically in .NET 8 — no migration is required for correctness.

That said, the idiomatic .NET 8 approach would be to replace raw threads with `Task.Run` + `CancellationToken` for cooperative cancellation:

```csharp
public class SimpleWorker
{
    public async Task RunBatchAsync(List<WorkItem> items, CancellationToken ct = default)
    {
        var tasks = items.Select(item => Task.Run(() => Process(item, ct), ct));
        await Task.WhenAll(tasks);
    }

    private void Process(WorkItem item, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
    }
}
```

**Summary:** No `Thread.Abort` to fix here. Your code will compile and run on .NET 8 as-is. Consider modernizing to tasks/cancellation tokens for better resource usage and graceful shutdown support.
